Rewritten: the decision core the plugin calls Jev. hermes_switchyard/client.py
(the TypeSafe System One and OpenRouter Decisions endpoint, its payload of a
state and typed questions, its answer shape, its retry policy), routing.py and
two_stage_routing.py (the choice over skill candidates with an explicit none
option and its needs_skill Noul, the criteria built from candidate names, the
0.80 needs/winning/confidence thresholds, the 4,000-character task and
200-candidate bounds) and reasoning_effort_adapter.py (the effort levels and
the rubric for each, and the rule that the caller's level is the cap) are
reimplemented in Common Lisp as one on-demand decision: the switchyard:route
verb, the switchyard:skills list and the switchyard_route tool. The request
wording is the plugin's, with the two questions asked once instead of fanned
out over four partitions.

Left out, because a Nodecode cell's own seams already own them: the plugin's
lifecycle hooks (its automatic pre_llm_call skill recommendation, adaptive
effort middleware, tool gating, receipts, compaction and stuck detection),
which fired on its own schedule -- here the model or the operator asks, and
the answer is advisory; the two-stage fan-out and its caching/deadline
plumbing; the DOM computer-use and browser tools (a cell that drives a
browser is a different cell, and nodecode-chrome already does it); the
session-search rerank; the smart-approval provider; the legacy-cleanup
command; and the plugin's own configuration surface, of which this cell keeps
the provider, endpoint, model, effort cap and timeout.
